{#
	What the people running this installation have to say (XIV-120, §8.16).

	One card per notice, above everything else on the page, because a maintenance
	window on Sunday is information about whether the work below it can be done at
	all. The widget is only on the page when there is something on it —
	`App\Dashboard\Widget\NoticeWidget` returns null otherwise — so this template
	never draws an empty state and never says "no announcements".

	**It is deliberately plain.** No image, no accent colour, no icon competing for
	attention, no call to action. An operator writing to every customer of an ERP
	they depend on is doing something serious, and the visual grammar here is a
	dated announcement rather than a marketing card: a title, who wrote it, when,
	and what it says.

	The body is printed with `nl2br` over escaped text, which is the whole of the
	formatting on offer. Markdown, links and HTML were all considered and all
	refused: the moment a notice can contain a link it is a channel somebody can
	be phished through, on the one screen in the product a customer has no reason
	to distrust.
#}
{% for notice in notices %}
	<article class="card mb-3">
		<div class="card-body">
			<div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2 justify-content-between align-items-start">
				<h3 class="h6 card-title mb-1">{{ notice.title }}</h3>

				{#
					Dismissing is a POST because it writes a row, and the row goes
					into this customer's own database — §4.4 gives a customer-facing
					instance no write privilege in the control plane, which is where
					the notice itself lives. See `App\Tenant\Entity\NoticeDismissal`.

					Per person, not per company: somebody putting this away has read
					it, and nobody should be able to take a maintenance window off a
					colleague's screen.
				#}
				<form method="post" action="{{ path('notice_dismiss', {id: notice.id}) }}">
					<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('notice-dismiss') }}">
					<button type="submit" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary">
						{{ 'notice.dismiss'|trans }}
					</button>
				</form>
			</div>

			{#
				The date and the author, which is what makes this an announcement
				rather than an advertisement — and the author is a name copied onto
				the notice when it was published, because this reader's instance
				cannot read the `operator` table at all (§4.4) and a join would be
				unreadable by the very party the column is for.
			#}
			<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-2">
				<time datetime="{{ notice.publishedAt|date('c') }}">{{ notice.publishedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</time>
				· {{ notice.authorLabel }}
			</p>

			<p class="card-text mb-0">{{ notice.body|nl2br }}</p>
		</div>
	</article>
{% endfor %}
